WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:01.350
ABBY CAREY: Hey, Martin.

00:00:01.350 --> 00:00:02.936
How's it going?

00:00:02.936 --> 00:00:04.019
MARTIN OMANDER: Hey, Abby.

00:00:04.019 --> 00:00:06.720
Well, I'm having a European wine
party with a group of friends

00:00:06.720 --> 00:00:10.170
soon, and we each want to buy a
vintage wine from a small wine

00:00:10.170 --> 00:00:11.430
store in Paris.

00:00:11.430 --> 00:00:13.770
The website shows
how many euros it is,

00:00:13.770 --> 00:00:16.695
but it doesn't
convert to US dollars.

00:00:16.695 --> 00:00:18.870
ABBY CAREY: You know, I
think we could build you

00:00:18.870 --> 00:00:20.760
a new tool with Python
that'll save you

00:00:20.760 --> 00:00:22.590
and your friends
some conversion time.

00:00:22.590 --> 00:00:23.701
I'll show you how.

00:00:23.701 --> 00:00:25.625
[THEME MUSIC]

00:00:33.145 --> 00:00:35.520
ABBY CAREY: Python is one of
the most popular programming

00:00:35.520 --> 00:00:37.450
languages and growing.

00:00:37.450 --> 00:00:39.610
It's well suited for
a number of use cases,

00:00:39.610 --> 00:00:43.220
including web applications,
machine learning, and big data.

00:00:43.220 --> 00:00:45.720
Cloud Run lets you use
any runtime you want,

00:00:45.720 --> 00:00:49.080
making it easy to deploy
Python in a serverless way.

00:00:49.080 --> 00:00:51.113
Cloud Run is also
fully managed, meaning

00:00:51.113 --> 00:00:53.280
you don't have to worry
about infrastructure scaling

00:00:53.280 --> 00:00:56.310
if your service starts
getting a ton of traffic.

00:00:56.310 --> 00:00:57.870
MARTIN OMANDER:
That sounds awesome.

00:00:57.870 --> 00:00:59.690
Do I have to change
my Python code

00:00:59.690 --> 00:01:02.235
to get it to run on Cloud Run?

00:01:02.235 --> 00:01:04.610
ABBY CAREY: If you have an
existing stateless Python app,

00:01:04.610 --> 00:01:06.870
all you need to do is
add one file to deploy

00:01:06.870 --> 00:01:08.090
a surface to Cloud Run.

00:01:08.090 --> 00:01:09.890
No code changes needed.

00:01:09.890 --> 00:01:11.930
We like to use Flask
for small services

00:01:11.930 --> 00:01:14.150
like this because it's a
lightweight framework that's

00:01:14.150 --> 00:01:15.770
easy to set up.

00:01:15.770 --> 00:01:18.230
Our service has
the basics, an HTML

00:01:18.230 --> 00:01:22.580
file where we create a form to
get user input, a simple CSS

00:01:22.580 --> 00:01:26.120
file, and an app.py
file where we set routes

00:01:26.120 --> 00:01:28.028
and define functions.

00:01:28.028 --> 00:01:28.820
MARTIN OMANDER: OK.

00:01:28.820 --> 00:01:32.090
That looks exactly like the
web apps I've built before.

00:01:32.090 --> 00:01:35.008
What is that extra file
I need for Cloud Run?

00:01:35.008 --> 00:01:36.800
ABBY CAREY: The last
file we need to define

00:01:36.800 --> 00:01:38.220
is the Docker file.

00:01:38.220 --> 00:01:41.030
This allows us to customize
the runtime of our container

00:01:41.030 --> 00:01:43.010
to suit our needs exactly.

00:01:43.010 --> 00:01:45.740
Containers are a way to
isolate our application

00:01:45.740 --> 00:01:48.500
to make it run the same no
matter where it's deployed.

00:01:48.500 --> 00:01:50.240
When creating a
Docker file, we first

00:01:50.240 --> 00:01:53.930
need to specify a base Docker
image with the front command.

00:01:53.930 --> 00:01:56.780
This is where we set
our Python runtime.

00:01:56.780 --> 00:01:59.240
The copy command adds files
from your Docker client's

00:01:59.240 --> 00:02:00.830
current directory.

00:02:00.830 --> 00:02:04.670
The run command installs
Flask, gunicorn, and currency

00:02:04.670 --> 00:02:07.220
converter dependencies
for our service.

00:02:07.220 --> 00:02:10.880
And finally, CMD is a command
to start our application

00:02:10.880 --> 00:02:13.730
inside the container
and binds it to a port.

00:02:13.730 --> 00:02:16.520
The app app at the end
means import our app

00:02:16.520 --> 00:02:18.008
from the app.py file.

00:02:18.008 --> 00:02:19.550
MARTIN OMANDER: What
if I want to use

00:02:19.550 --> 00:02:21.623
a different version of Python?

00:02:21.623 --> 00:02:23.540
ABBY CAREY: One of the
advantages of Cloud Run

00:02:23.540 --> 00:02:25.460
is that you can run
any Python version you

00:02:25.460 --> 00:02:29.130
want as long as there is a base
Docker image available for it.

00:02:29.130 --> 00:02:32.240
I can even use the newest
version of Python, version 3.8,

00:02:32.240 --> 00:02:33.470
if I want to.

00:02:33.470 --> 00:02:35.000
Now that we have
our Docker file,

00:02:35.000 --> 00:02:37.070
we can build our container
with Cloud Build.

00:02:37.070 --> 00:02:39.320
This bundles up our code
along with everything

00:02:39.320 --> 00:02:41.570
we've added in our
Docker file and pushes it

00:02:41.570 --> 00:02:43.400
to the Container
Registry, a place

00:02:43.400 --> 00:02:45.380
to store container images.

00:02:45.380 --> 00:02:48.810
And finally, we deploy
the service to Cloud Run.

00:02:48.810 --> 00:02:51.180
It only takes two commands
to get the service out

00:02:51.180 --> 00:02:52.170
to the world.

00:02:52.170 --> 00:02:54.510
In our terminal, we
first build the container

00:02:54.510 --> 00:02:56.100
using the builds command.

00:02:56.100 --> 00:02:58.440
And then we deploy the
service using the container

00:02:58.440 --> 00:03:00.270
image we just built.

00:03:00.270 --> 00:03:02.610
If we check out the Cloud
Run section of Google Cloud

00:03:02.610 --> 00:03:05.790
console, we now see
our Cloud Run service.

00:03:05.790 --> 00:03:07.380
If we click the
service, we can see

00:03:07.380 --> 00:03:11.070
important info, like metrics
and the URL of our service.

00:03:11.070 --> 00:03:13.440
We can also redirect
or split user traffic

00:03:13.440 --> 00:03:16.050
to previous revisions if
we discover our latest

00:03:16.050 --> 00:03:17.897
revision has a breaking change.

00:03:17.897 --> 00:03:18.730
MARTIN OMANDER: Wow.

00:03:18.730 --> 00:03:20.130
This sounds great.

00:03:20.130 --> 00:03:23.200
Let me test it out.

00:03:23.200 --> 00:03:24.210
Oh, wait.

00:03:24.210 --> 00:03:26.070
I think I'm doing
something wrong.

00:03:26.070 --> 00:03:28.530
I'm being taken
to an error page.

00:03:28.530 --> 00:03:30.807
How can I find out
what's going on?

00:03:30.807 --> 00:03:33.390
ABBY CAREY: That's where Cloud
Monitoring comes to the rescue.

00:03:33.390 --> 00:03:35.370
Everything that happens
within this service

00:03:35.370 --> 00:03:38.410
is logged in Cloud
Monitoring, including errors.

00:03:38.410 --> 00:03:39.900
You can see the
logs of our service

00:03:39.900 --> 00:03:42.810
from the Cloud
Monitoring-powered Logs tab.

00:03:42.810 --> 00:03:46.140
I think this is showing a
little too much info though.

00:03:46.140 --> 00:03:47.820
If I had to the
Metrics tab, I can

00:03:47.820 --> 00:03:50.760
see the latest errors filtering
out all the other noise.

00:03:50.760 --> 00:03:52.320
Let's take a look.

00:03:52.320 --> 00:03:53.430
MARTIN OMANDER: Hmm.

00:03:53.430 --> 00:03:59.892
Looks like an error value
was triggered on line 20.

00:03:59.892 --> 00:04:00.740
ABBY CAREY: Ah!

00:04:00.740 --> 00:04:02.990
I didn't account for
submitting an empty string.

00:04:02.990 --> 00:04:05.570
Let's fix that, build,
and deploy one more time.

00:04:09.240 --> 00:04:09.740
Ah.

00:04:09.740 --> 00:04:10.790
That should do the trick.

00:04:10.790 --> 00:04:11.420
MARTIN OMANDER: Awesome.

00:04:11.420 --> 00:04:12.770
Looks like it's now working.

00:04:12.770 --> 00:04:17.149
Let me see how much this
wine is going to cost me.

00:04:17.149 --> 00:04:19.370
Oh, I think I need to
take some money out

00:04:19.370 --> 00:04:21.450
of my savings account.

00:04:21.450 --> 00:04:23.300
ABBY CAREY: Oh, no.

00:04:23.300 --> 00:04:24.860
I'm sure it'll be
worth it, though.

00:04:24.860 --> 00:04:26.720
MARTIN OMANDER: But,
anyways, thanks.

00:04:26.720 --> 00:04:29.070
I can't wait to share this
service with my friends.

00:04:29.070 --> 00:04:30.848
I think it'll come in handy.

00:04:30.848 --> 00:04:33.390
ABBY CAREY: If you want to check
out the code for this video,

00:04:33.390 --> 00:04:34.800
look in the description below.

00:04:34.800 --> 00:04:35.780
Until next time.

00:04:35.780 --> 00:04:38.530
[THEME MUSIC]

